## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Extend each hosted dashboard project page from aggregate current-state counts into a useful,
read-only view of the project's durable Tool Shed work. The operator should be able to see Ideas,
Project Maps, Program Roadmaps (PRMs), and Campaigns; understand which are active, blocked,
completed, superseded, or otherwise terminal; follow their Idea → Map → PRM → Campaign
relationships; and review what changed over time.

This requires more than a template change. Reporter contract v1 sends aggregate counts and one
`last_completed_id`, while its retained material events carry generic summary codes without an
artifact identity. It does not report PRMs or Maps at all. A new privacy-bounded contract must
therefore provide both a current lifecycle inventory and artifact-specific lifecycle history.

### Why It Matters

The dashboard currently answers only "is anything open?" When a project is healthy and fully
reconciled, every count becomes zero and the page loses the very context the operator needs: what
was delivered, which Idea produced it, which PRM governed it, which Campaigns completed it, and how
the loop closed. This makes a successful project look empty and forces the operator back into each
workspace to understand even basic project history.

### Desired Outcome

From one authenticated project page, the operator can:

- see a compact lifecycle inventory grouped or filtered by Ideas, Maps, PRMs, and Campaigns;
- distinguish current, blocked, completed, superseded, deferred, abandoned, and other controlled
  lifecycle states without treating zero open work as zero history;
- expand or traverse the durable parent chain from an Idea through its Map and PRM to Campaigns;
- see stable IDs, useful display titles, current lifecycle, outcome/reconciliation state,
  freshness, and reporting-instance source;
- review a chronological recent-history feed of artifact-specific lifecycle transitions;
- receive live SSE refresh when a new semantic snapshot or lifecycle transition reaches the hosted
  database.

Completed inventory remains visible as durable project context while transition history may use a
bounded retention window. Hosted state stays advisory and never mutates or replaces the local Tool
Shed database.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Local Tool Shed state remains authoritative; the hosted dashboard is an authenticated,
  read-only advisory projection.
- Extend the existing versioned allowlist. Do not upload document bodies, prompts, source, paths,
  commands, raw diagnostics, credentials, or arbitrary event text.
- Multiple enrolled instances for one project may disagree or report at different times. The UI
  must preserve source and freshness rather than silently merging divergent lifecycle truth.
- Stable IDs, controlled artifact types and lifecycle states, timestamps, relationships, and
  reconciliation summaries are suitable bounded fields. Display titles need an explicit length,
  sanitization, and privacy decision.
- Keep public documentation routes and the existing dashboard shell compatible.
- A semantic report update should trigger the existing SSE refresh; heartbeat-only reports must
  not create history or reload loops.
- Do not add remote work control, approval, artifact editing, mitigation, or lifecycle mutation.
- Do not mirror the complete local SQLite database into PostgreSQL.
- Do not turn every one-minute report into a history entry.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Render the existing generic material events | Smallest UI change; provides a basic activity feed | Cannot identify an Idea, PRM, or Campaign and cannot reconstruct status/history | Useful only as a temporary supplement |
| Add a bounded lifecycle inventory snapshot | Directly answers what exists and its current status; server can derive counts | Requires report-contract, ingestion, storage, and multi-instance semantics | Preferred foundation |
| Add artifact-specific lifecycle transition events | Provides meaningful history without minute-by-minute noise | Requires stable idempotency, retention, and transition extraction | Preferred alongside the snapshot |
| Upload a full local database/document mirror | Maximum detail | Violates minimum disclosure, creates competing authority, increases coupling and recovery risk | Reject |

The minimum complete direction is one idempotent bounded inventory plus change-only transition
events. The project page can then offer a Work view with type/status filters and relationship
drilldown, plus a Recent History view or section. Counts should be derived from the accepted hosted
inventory so cards and rows cannot disagree.

## Open Questions

- Should completed inventory be unbounded for all durable lifecycle artifacts, or bounded by a
  count/time window with explicit truncation and local-review guidance?
- Should display titles be reported, or should the first privacy-minimal release use stable IDs
  only? IDs are safe but materially less usable.
- When several instances report the same project, should the default show the freshest complete
  snapshot, separate per-instance views, or a divergence warning with operator selection?
- Which outcome fields belong beside each artifact: lifecycle only, reconciliation state and
  disposition, or also the nearest open owning loop?
- Should Project Maps appear as a first-class filter even though the initial operator request named
  Ideas, PRMs, and Campaigns? They are necessary to display the actual ownership chain.
- Is recent history best as a fifth project tab, a section under Work, or a filterable combined
  Work/History surface?
- What payload and row bounds cover large repositories without silently presenting a partial view?

## Decisions

- This is a new extension Idea, not a reopening of completed dashboard origin `IDEA-0007`.
- The target experience must preserve completed work and history; a healthy zero-count summary is
  not sufficient.
- Proper artifact visibility requires a reporter-contract revision and hosted persistence, not
  only template work.
- Prefer a bounded lifecycle snapshot plus artifact-specific change events. Reject database or
  document mirroring.
- Preserve local authority, authenticated read-only hosting, explicit instance source/freshness,
  and semantic SSE refresh.

## Don't Forget

- The hosted database already retains generic `MaterialEvent` rows, but they lack artifact IDs and
  cannot satisfy this outcome by themselves.
- Reporter v1 reads active campaigns, active ideas, and completed campaigns only for aggregate
  state; it does not report Maps or PRMs and sends no artifact list.
- Completed and superseded artifacts are essential product information, not clutter to hide when
  current work reaches zero.
- History should represent lifecycle changes, not receipt cadence or repeated unchanged snapshots.

